/*
 * EmitsUnwrap.ag — §6's `x!` and the trap it means, as emitted.
 *
 * Copyright © 2026 Kyle J. Cardoza. MIT licence (see docs/LICENSE).
 *
 * `x!` is the ONE place the language has runtime behaviour, and §6 says so
 * outright: "the raw value, trapped if it is nil … §0's exception: ObjC has no
 * 'crash if null', so `x!` is compiled to a null check that aborts."
 *
 * This specimen pins both halves, because either alone is a silent wrong answer:
 *
 *   - the emitted HEADER carries the macro — and, as the other specimens show by
 *     NOT carrying it, only when the unit unwraps something. §2's specimen is a
 *     byte-for-byte golden, so a macro block in every header would have changed
 *     the one output the language is specified by.
 *   - the emitted BODY actually calls it: `return STERLING_UNWRAP(self.name);`.
 *     The predecessor printed the operand alone — `self.name` — which COMPILES
 *     and drops the trap, which is why it was refused by name until there was
 *     somewhere to emit the trap to.
 *
 * The trap itself is a builtin and not an assert, for the reason §6 gives: an
 * `NDEBUG`-stripped `NSAssert` "would silently make `!` non-trapping in an
 * optimised build". That is measurable and was measured — the emitted macro, cut
 * out of the generated header and compiled on its own, survives a non-nil operand
 * and dies with SIGILL on a nil one, at every optimisation level.
 *
 * `shoutTwice` is here for §6's precedence rule: "postfix, binding tighter than
 * the message send, so `x!.foo` is `[(x!) foo]`". Nothing in the emitter arranges
 * that — the send's receiver goes through the unwrap case and comes out wrapped —
 * and a golden is the only place the arrangement is visible.
 */
class Holder: Object {
    property name: String?

    method shout() -> String {
        return self.name!;
    }

    method shoutTwice() -> String {
        return self.name!.uppercaseString();
    }
}
